{"title":"2011 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 23","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Answer_Choices)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Answer Choices)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(similar_to_1)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (similar to 1)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7_(backwards_solution_3)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7 (backwards solution 3)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Seven students count from 1 to 1000 as follows:\n</p><p>Alice says all the numbers, except she skips the middle number in each consecutive group of three numbers. That is, Alice says 1, 3, 4, 6, 7, 9, . . ., 997, 999, 1000.\n</p><p>Barbara says all of the numbers that Alice doesn't say, except she also skips the middle number in each consecutive group of three numbers.\n</p><p>Candice says all of the numbers that neither Alice nor Barbara says, except she also skips the middle number in each consecutive group of three numbers.\n</p><p>Debbie, Eliza, and Fatima say all of the numbers that none of the students with the first names beginning before theirs in the alphabet say, except each also skips the middle number in each of her consecutive groups of three numbers.\n</p><p>Finally, George says the only number that no one else says.\n</p><p>What number does George say?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/2/8920630c3afdd46de5fdac3d771769ffe9042df7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 37\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 242\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 365\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 728\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 998$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"430\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First look at the numbers Alice says. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/c/79c9a5bb2a4062fb9535c9de974c54b89aba9c99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 3, 4, 6, 7, 9 \\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"117\" height=\"16\" /> skipping every number that is congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/9/14993088c90e11f704b76e68891cac3a83f0389a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\pmod 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" />. Thus, Barbara says those numbers EXCEPT every second - being <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/2/062645807c2390febd32f01020d4419621eb2275.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 + 3^1 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{3^2=9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"192\" height=\"19\" />. So Barbara skips every number congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/0/320fd61a5704824ad96a46764d2ee9d136d4e230.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\pmod 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" />. We continue and see: \n</p><p>Alice skips <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/9/14993088c90e11f704b76e68891cac3a83f0389a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\pmod 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" />, Barbara skips <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/0/320fd61a5704824ad96a46764d2ee9d136d4e230.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\pmod 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" />, Candice skips <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/2/f72b020c86dcbddbff0056be47af758740d84f9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$14 \\pmod {27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />, Debbie skips <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/a/3fa51dbe2affab5c2d481f7fe4db0aeb62d659ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$41 \\pmod {81}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />, Eliza skips <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/d/9ed7034fb3ba4e6be262b69c0041e741b37b7251.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$122 \\pmod {243}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"116\" height=\"18\" />, and Fatima skips <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/3/133d651fc99bca8646cf58ea5d3edc12500ff553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$365 \\pmod {729}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"116\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Since the only number congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/3/133d651fc99bca8646cf58ea5d3edc12500ff553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$365 \\pmod {729}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"116\" height=\"18\" /> and less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/2/a7211efdf41d2bc909e3d0549b2ab5cba0356ca5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"17\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/8/988ab73be88b10c8cbf520db5e7cb4b3c6531963.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$365$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />, the correct answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/f/85fe11bf0663d2377c32a8b0a1c728d8bd460afd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{365\\ \\mathbf{(C)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"73\" height=\"29\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>After Alice says all her numbers, the numbers not mentioned yet are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/5/c254a439f1b7c61678f6c93b65a1e804cb43a022.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Alice: } 2,5,8,11,14,17,\\cdots,998.\\]\" width=\"244\" height=\"16\" />After Barbara says all her numbers, the numbers that haven't been said yet are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/9/5398b79586eb3a31db84fcb7401958793bc617ef.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Barbara: } 5,14,23,32,41,50,\\cdots,995.\\]\" width=\"284\" height=\"16\" />After Candice, the list is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/0/c70eae04acd2e5039b7f3dd4d2f8082f4232fd49.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Candice: } 14,41,68,\\cdots,986.\\]\" width=\"214\" height=\"16\" />Notice how each list is an arithmetic sequence where the common differene is thrice the common ratio of the previous list and the first term is the second term of the previous list. Now that the pattern is clear, we construct the rest of the lists: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/f/94f813642162b02d347eec913ee07568faaf9fa6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Debbie: } 41,122,203,\\cdots,959\\]\" width=\"220\" height=\"16\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/c/cec87f92101e13fd4c468c7b7d4b709564697123.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Eliza: } 122,365,608,878\\]\" width=\"180\" height=\"16\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/f/e5fcf7b4b0cb7db4e8d4e2d32e86da8d151e08c3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Fatima: } 365\\]\" width=\"89\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Thus, George says <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/9/4c90e2c2dd383b488d045b341bdabc8cf0241524.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 365}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"74\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that Alice has skipped the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/e/68eabcc9922cdddf961b463f94a8b64d0fecbd42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/7/0c71dca8822a567176a78d67d8327e83a47ccfbd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1,2,3,...,333$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"136\" height=\"16\" />.\nNamely, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/4/674e3d30f059c1abd0e9efdfdeabb6a5f5917a0a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3\\cdot1-1,3\\cdot2-1,3\\cdot3-1,...,3\\cdot333-1\\]\" width=\"314\" height=\"16\" />\nThus the numbers that Barbara skips are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/e/49ed09af990c89a4bd31a46fec217927c1ab01b4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3\\cdot2-1,3\\cdot5-1,3\\cdot8-1,...\\]\" width=\"225\" height=\"16\" />\nor in a more general expression, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/4/91414dcd27e1e7bf2d11a9f27488db1bb19eef50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3(3n-1)-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"18\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/8/e88831557651694d152b65db1ff4b5fd2d5e237b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1,2,3,...$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"100\" height=\"16\" />.\nNamely, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/f/f4fe7662f52581f068a0b6bff96a799094169d58.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3\\cdot(3\\cdot1-1)-1,3\\cdot(3\\cdot2-1)-1,3\\cdot(3\\cdot3-1)-1,...\\]\" width=\"428\" height=\"18\" />\nRepeating the pattern until George, we have the first number he says,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/6/796d7f9cb0796bd989119b1c57d66c12ab5d9c00.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3(3(3(3(3(3\\cdot1-1)-1)-1)-1)-1)-1= \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 365}\\]\" width=\"433\" height=\"28\" />\nIn addition, note that the second number George says exceeds <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee927e1332358c96c62c277441c907c4f51057f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Answer_Choices.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Answer_Choices)\">Solution 4 (Answer Choices)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Answer Choices)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Similar to Solution 1 we find that the only number that George can say must leave a remainder of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> when divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and that it must also leave a remainder of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> when divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Since we as human beings are usually lazy (we mean smart), and that MAA provides answer choices, we check all the possible numbers and find that our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/9/4c90e2c2dd383b488d045b341bdabc8cf0241524.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 365}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"74\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Every integer from 1 to 1000 can be written in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/9/9795df69bc2c4b031fb8b67d1ccf3966a7290dde.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"17\" /> in base 10, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/9/2e9bb94664d3e2d8c57a28afbd35f239ab99ef27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is a trinary integer with no more than 7 significant digits. The important insight is that person <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/b/dbbd240a1f33f450fe3b013f6cbabeaee78184b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le{k}\\le6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" /> will not say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> if and only if the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />th digit from the right of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/9/2e9bb94664d3e2d8c57a28afbd35f239ab99ef27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is 1. Therefore, the last 6 digits of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/9/2e9bb94664d3e2d8c57a28afbd35f239ab99ef27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> must be 1, as the first 6 people never said the number. The only options for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/9/2e9bb94664d3e2d8c57a28afbd35f239ab99ef27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> are thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0ded33403f4bda6795a013c2de0b390bb6b7c2e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2111111$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"62\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/3/76372fba52646489ed4e9aa4b921ab15b2a4cadf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1111111$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"62\" height=\"12\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/e/bee6035dc11bedee47b49a8038d244d3c22ab7bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0111111$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"62\" height=\"13\" />. But, since George only said one number, the first two must have been too big for it to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/f/d1f86ae57dffc365f1d437160162158c84db3c3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\le1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"15\" />. Our answer is therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/7182a5e7d4f5d59da34ebe7b74b8f8aedf1381de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$111111_3+1=\\frac{3^6-1}{3-1}+1=364+1=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 365}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"383\" height=\"42\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28similar_to_1.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(similar_to_1)\">Solution 6 (similar to 1)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (similar to 1)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We see that Alice excludes the middle numbers in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/2/7b22df6e1aa6e52c571e72eda9035fc75d77dfb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,\\cdots.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"189\" height=\"17\" /> She excludes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/f/c9fa96fb5ba9e3f455de01d4c1add0c60ca6960f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\mod3.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"78\" height=\"14\" /> Barbara excludes the middle numbers in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/c/ccc8a7fb0e0bfa9426ca84fe860cba521dcc8763.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2,5,8,11,14,17,20,\\cdots.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"191\" height=\"17\" /> She excludes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/e/37ece7589632f88d1dea44b2697051b8326cbb3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5\\mod9.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"78\" height=\"14\" /> Let each mod be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/d/d8db61141f3769e4d46aeb535756d75f4543ab0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a\\mod b.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"77\" height=\"14\" /> We notice that, each time, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> triples, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/c/a8cd9b42586f30d043312430933ffd844f96978e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=\\dfrac{b+1}2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"39\" /> We can guess that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/a/83ad96d22f37773114fd550dbd82d2bcd95e51fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$729$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> is about the right size for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> must be an exponent of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />); any larger, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> wouldn't be less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/4/6b434ebc2071e706df740975a3c90aad3cfa1455.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"13\" /> (We could also count: abcdef is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/4/7d440ada8a3f3b7ea6f5beb66cb1834ededc2399.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^6$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" />)\nThus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/e/c0e2185992e2be557f820b3a7b2623da27799209.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=\\dfrac{729+1}2=365$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"150\" height=\"38\" />\n~Technodoggo\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7_.28backwards_solution_3.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7_(backwards_solution_3)\">Solution 7 (backwards solution 3)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7 (backwards solution 3)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We work backwards to find the placement of George's number in every person's set as a function of the place in the next person's set. Let George's number be the item number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> in his set (obviously <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />). Then Fatima places sets of three around every number in George's set and subtracts one to find the item number of George's number in her set (now <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/d/55df35f489e7df2ca6ff0886b2d6f3b23b4e98b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\cdot 1-1=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"98\" height=\"15\" />). This process repeats five more times for a total of six times.\n</p><p>We apply the function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/7/7f7f398378fbbd8d49403f8aa5616e86123796e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)=3x-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" /> six times to attain the sequence of numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/4/1a429e6267f84c15c0e1c30d2afe9913908b6be1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,5,14,41,122,\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\;365}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"213\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~eevee9406\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A\" title=\"2011 AMC 10A\">2011 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2011 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2011 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2011 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 22\">Problem 22</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2011 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 24\">Problem 24</a></b>\n